## Lemon Law Coverage for Pre-Owned Vehicles

California lemon law extends to both new and used vehicles - sold with a warranty.

## Understanding Warranties on Used Vehicles from Dealerships

Used car dealerships in California commonly sell vehicles with a dealer warranty. These are generally valid for 30 days after the purchase date or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer.

If your vehicle exhibits one or more defects within these terms, you may be eligible for the same California lemon law benefits as a new vehicle.

Your Rights as a Consumer Under California Lemon Law

Auto dealerships are required to repair any defects covered under the terms of the warranty. If the certified repair facility is unable to repair the defect, you may be eligible to initiate a California lemon law buyback - which includes the cost of the vehicle, official expenses _associated_ with the vehicle, incidental costs, and all legal and attorney fees.

The Implications of Buying a Vehicle “As Is”

Buying a vehicle “as is” means you’re agreeing to take on any problems the vehicle might encounter after you’ve purchased it, potentially reducing your coverage under Aliso Viejo lemon law.

For an “as is” sale to hold up legally, this stipulation needs to be explicitly stated and evident on the buyer’s guide when the vehicle is being showcased at the dealership. Should there be a failure to properly disclose this information, you might still be eligible to pursue a claim under the California lemon law.

